{"name":"Habañero Gold","hex":"#fecf3c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"habañero","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["(hypercorrect) Nonstandard form of habanero."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/haba%C3%B1ero"},{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"}],"entries":[{"title":"Habanero","blocks":[{"text":"The habanero is a pungent cultivar of Capsicum chinense chili pepper. Unripe habaneros are green, and they color as they mature. The most common color variants are orange and red, but the fruit may also be white, brown, yellow, green, or purple. Typically, a ripe habanero is 2–6 centimetres long. Habanero chilis are very hot, rated 100,000–350,000 on the Scoville scale. The habanero's heat, flavor, and floral aroma make it a common ingredient in hot sauces and other spicy foods."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Habanero_closeup_edit2.jpg/330px-Habanero_closeup_edit2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Habanero","wikidata":"Q764375"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/ExpoIndigenous2015_065.JPG/500px-ExpoIndigenous2015_065.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/ExpoIndigenous2015_065.JPG/1280px-ExpoIndigenous2015_065.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/ExpoIndigenous2015_065.JPG/500px-ExpoIndigenous2015_065.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Habanero hot sauce from Flor de Lirio, an Indigenous cooperative in Peto Municipality, Yucatán","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AExpoIndigenous2015_065.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Habanero_cut_in_half_on_a_blue_plate.jpg/500px-Habanero_cut_in_half_on_a_blue_plate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Habanero_cut_in_half_on_a_blue_plate.jpg/1280px-Habanero_cut_in_half_on_a_blue_plate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Habanero_cut_in_half_on_a_blue_plate.jpg/500px-Habanero_cut_in_half_on_a_blue_plate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A habanero pepper cut in half to expose the seeds","link":"https://en.wikiped